1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,480
Tank top season means your underarms are out - and if they look darker than the rest of your skin, it's probably not your imagination.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:12,960
Dermatology clinics see this complaint constantly, and it usually comes down to three everyday habits.

3
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:16,560
First, shaving with a dull blade or against the grain.

4
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:27,040
That tugging motion irritates the hair follicle, and skin responds to repeated irritation by producing extra melanin - the same defense reaction that darkens a healing scrape.

5
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:33,760
Second, fragrance and dyes in antiperspirants and deodorants, especially when applied right after shaving.

6
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:41,920
Freshly shaved skin absorbs those ingredients more easily, so the irritation - and the pigment it leaves behind - builds up faster.

7
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:43,760
Third, tight workout gear.

8
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:52,720
Sports bras and compression tanks that trap sweat and rub the same spot during every workout create the same friction-driven pigmentation over time.

9
00:00:53,040 --> 00:01:01,360
Switch to a sharp single-blade razor and shave with the grain, pick a fragrance-free deodorant, and reach for looser, moisture-wicking fabric when you can.

10
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:05,040
Underarms usually lighten up fast once the irritation stops.
